\begin{abstract}
We investigate a spatial phytoplankton--zooplankton reaction--diffusion model motivated by ecological pattern formation in marine plankton systems. Building on earlier studies of plankton patchiness and biological wave propagation, including the work of Medvinsky et al. \cite{Medvinsky2001} and Garvie and Trenchea \cite{GarvieTrenchea2007}, we analyze the emergence of complex spatiotemporal dynamics driven by the interaction of local predator--prey kinetics and spatial dispersal. Linear stability analysis of the homogeneous coexistence equilibrium is used to identify diffusion-driven instabilities and the onset of Turing--Hopf bifurcation, where stationary spatial modes and temporal oscillations interact. Numerical simulations demonstrate that this codimension-two mechanism can generate rich ecological patterns, including rotating spiral waves, modulated wave trains, irregular patch dynamics, and spatiotemporal chaos. The results show that diffusion-mediated feedback between phytoplankton growth and zooplankton grazing can destabilize otherwise regular population oscillations and produce persistent heterogeneous structures resembling observed plankton blooms. Our study highlights the importance of Turing--Hopf interactions as an organizing mechanism for plankton pattern formation and provides a framework for understanding the transition from coherent spiral waves to chaotic spatiotemporal behavior in marine ecosystems.
\end{abstract}

